WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.950
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:02.700
- [Narrator] A seafood market,

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.350
a virology lab, and a mine
deep in the mountains.

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:09.140
Key events at these places
in China could be crucial

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:12.410
to understanding how a small
cluster of coronavirus cases

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:13.900
ended up sweeping the world.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.190
Because almost a year and a half

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:18.410
into this global health crisis,
politicians and scientists

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.490
are still debating how it all started.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:22.500
- [Betsy] If you're gonna look
for the origin of a pandemic,

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.210
you really should start right
away when the clues are fresh.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.910
Right now that's looking pretty difficult.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:29.360
- [Narrator] That's
because access to Wuhan

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.000
has been really limited.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.280
- Until you nail it down definitively,

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.020
you've always got to keep an open mind

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:37.890
that it might be something else.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:38.890
- [Narrator] The main hypothesis

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:41.170
is that the virus originated from bats.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:44.020
Jumped to another animal, then to us.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:44.950
And the leap to humans

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.430
might've happened here at this market.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:49.610
10 miles away is where
some scientists believe

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.523
that the virus may have
accidentally leaked from a lab.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:55.100
And both of these spots in Wuhan

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.920
have an increasingly important
connection to one place

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.740
that's over 1000 miles away

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:02.160
in the mountains of Yunnan province.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:03.650
So we visit these key places

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.010
to break down what we
know about the coronavirus

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.893
to understand how the global
pandemic may have started.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.200
Stop number one, the
Huanan Seafood Market,

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.960
December 31st, 2019.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.650
This is the day the WHO learned

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.250
about a cluster of
pneumonia cases in Wuhan.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.790
And increasingly, the
focus was on the market.

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:24.820
The complex spans an area that's the size

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:28.300
of nine football fields
and has over 600 stalls.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.350
About 10 of those were
selling wild animals

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.203
like bamboo rats,
hedgehogs and hog badgers.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:36.900
- [Betsy] These wildlife
are raised on farms,

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.730
sometimes in crowded conditions

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.780
where it's easy for animals
to spread viruses back

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:43.050
and forth between each other

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:47.020
and the viruses start to mix
and match and can change.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:47.853
- [Narrator] Some wildlife

00:01:47.853 --> 00:01:49.990
that are receptive to
coronavirus infections

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:51.550
are consumed in China,

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:54.170
either for food or used
in traditional medicine.

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:57.230
- [Betsy] These animals are
shipped to urban markets

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:00.900
where they are sold sometimes
live and butchered onsite.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:05.300
And all this contact with
live possibly infected animals

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.210
or the infected fresh meat

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:10.170
has become a major
concern in recent years.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:12.250
- [Narrator] And in Wuhan,
many of the first cases

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:14.360
were either vendors who
worked at the market,

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:15.993
or people who had shopped there.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:21.010
By January 1st, Chinese
authorities shut the place down.

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:23.430
- [Betsy] Well, originally
Chinese authorities did feel

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.100
that it possibly came
from the Huanan Market

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.763
and from wild meat of some sort.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:31.340
- [Narrator] About three weeks later,

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:33.560
scientists in Wuhan and Beijing published

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.350
a pivotal paper in the
medical journal, The Lancet.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:38.420
This chart shows how
some of the early cases

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:41.180
were people who hadn't been at the market.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:43.430
More than a year later, there was new data

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:45.800
to piece together a clearer picture,

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:46.970
that's because China allowed

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:49.820
a group of international
scientists to come to Wuhan

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:53.690
for several weeks to visit
the market and hospitals.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:57.200
- [Betsy] A team from the
WHO that visited China

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.090
also learned going through the data

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:01.260
that by mid to late December,

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.970
the viruses that were infecting people

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:05.530
were genetically different enough

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:09.040
that they knew it wasn't all
coming from the same place.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:10.410
- [Narrator] The WHO said the presence

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.250
of early cases not linked
to the market could suggest

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.800
that it was not the original
source of the outbreak.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.080
And increasingly, one place a team visited

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.430
is at the center of another hypothesis,

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:22.730
the Wuhan Institute of Virology.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:24.950
- This is a world-class Institute

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:27.260
with high security laboratories

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:30.720
and does a lot of work with
scientists outside of China,

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.670
very vibrant collaboration
before the pandemic.

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:38.240
They would travel to caves
where bats are known to roost

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.170
and take samples, and then
study them in their lab.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:43.310
- [Narrator] These bat
coronaviruses and pathogens

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:45.540
are cataloged in databases.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.070
The researchers used this
library of information

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:49.500
to compare it to blood samples

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.230
and oral swabs from patients in Wuhan.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.320
And about a month after
the market was closed,

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.780
these researchers discovered that COVID-19

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:00.880
had a 96.2% genetic match
with the bat coronavirus

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.603
that the institute had found back in 2013.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:06.530
Scientists around the world consider this

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:09.400
a breakthrough in the
search for COVID-19 source,

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:10.840
because it strongly indicated

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:13.700
that it could have originated in bats.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:17.600
- This laboratory studies bat
coronaviruses right in Wuhan,

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.550
was an obvious question,
could it have escaped?

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:23.170
And even the Institute's own
researchers asked that question

00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:25.210
and went and did their own research.

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:26.190
- [Narrator] As questions swirled

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:28.760
around a possible
accident at the institute,

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:30.710
it's top bat coronavirus expert said

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:32.800
the virus didn't leak from her labs,

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.980
and none of its staff have
tested positive for COVID-19.

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:38.120
- These are not the first times

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.240
that we've had a world exposed to virus

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.730
as a result of failures in a Chinese lab.

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:46.300
- Last year, what started out
as a basic scientific question

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:49.960
got politicized with a lot
of finger pointing at China

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:51.743
yet there was no evidence presented.

00:04:51.743 --> 00:04:54.073
(journalists chattering)

00:04:54.073 --> 00:04:55.570
- [Man] Anything sir.

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:57.640
- [Narrator] And there was
also limited access to data

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.330
during the WHO-led team's trip to Wuhan.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.700
One of the stops was
the virology institute.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.830
- They asked questions
and they had a tour,

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:06.970
but they didn't get any firsthand look

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:11.390
at databases, medical
records, any sort of raw data

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:12.730
which would have shed light

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:16.560
on whether the virus
could have escaped or not.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:17.530
- [Narrator] Almost a week later,

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:20.480
scientists summed up their
findings at a press conference.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:23.390
They said the spillover from
an intermediate host to humans

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:25.220
was likely to very likely,

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.330
and as for questions surrounding the lab.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:28.430
- The findings suggest

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:32.590
that the laboratory incident hypothesis

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.040
is extremely unlikely.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:39.030
- [Narrator] But later, the WHO chief said

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:42.140
the team didn't sufficiently
examine the lab hypothesis,

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:43.973
and called for a fuller probe.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.700
Three months later after
that press conference,

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:49.210
a group of leading virologists
and epidemiologists

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:51.170
wrote a letter saying
there were no findings

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:52.300
in clear support of either

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:55.170
a natural spillover or a lab accident.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.720
- This group of people is not saying

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.450
that they believe it came from a lab.

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:01.670
What they're saying

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:04.490
is that there's not enough
evidence to rule it out.

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:07.310
Scientists investigate hypotheses

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:09.050
and they rule them out one by one.

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.690
And they just said that process
hasn't been able to happen

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:12.843
and it needs to happen.

00:06:12.843 --> 00:06:15.950
(speaking in foreign language)

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:17.730
- [Narrator] China says
it's cooperated fully

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:20.340
with the effort to search
for COVID-19's source

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:21.173
and has urged the WHO

00:06:21.173 --> 00:06:24.483
to investigate early
cases in other countries.

00:06:25.749 --> 00:06:26.840
(bright music)

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:28.770
And one place that could
provide some new evidence

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:31.760
for both hypotheses, Yunnan.

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:33.750
It's China's Southwestern most province

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:36.260
and home to many
coronavirus-carrying bats.

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:38.330
And could set the stage
for where the investigation

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:40.800
into the origin of the
virus could go next.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:45.640
- It's clearly an area where
coronaviruses are circulating,

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:49.160
including coronaviruses that
are very closely related

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:52.940
to SARS, the epidemic in 2003.

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:53.773
- [Narrator] Researchers

00:06:53.773 --> 00:06:56.060
from the Wuhan Institute
of Virology went to Yunnan

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:59.330
to study large bat
populations inside caves.

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:02.510
In these crowded spaces, viral
strains can mix together,

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:04.580
and become the building
blocks of coronavirus

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:06.400
that then jump to humans.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:07.233
- [Betsy] And at the same time,

00:07:07.233 --> 00:07:09.330
there are wildlife farms there

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:11.910
that supply animals to Wuhan.

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:14.230
- [Narrator] Scientists say,
this could explain how a virus

00:07:14.230 --> 00:07:16.770
from a bat cave leapt
to an intermediate host

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:18.370
then made its way to the market.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:21.880
There was also a mine in
Yunnan where scientists

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:25.230
from the institute spent
some time from 2012 onwards.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:27.700
It's where six miners who'd
been clearing bat droppings

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:31.040
had gotten sick, and developed
an unexplained pneumonia.

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:32.273
Three of them died.

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:37.010
So in 2013, scientists from
the Institute went to the cave

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:39.000
to collect samples from bats.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.160
A virus from one of
these samples turned out

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.790
to be that 96.2% genetic match

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:46.910
that was discovered by the
institute, inhaled as a big break

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:48.850
during the early days of the pandemic.

00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:50.040
It took months for the institute

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:52.030
to reveal that the bat coronavirus samples

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:54.643
were from the mine, and
that there were sick miners.

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:57.590
- The failure to describe it more

00:07:57.590 --> 00:07:59.630
became kind of a touchpoint

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:02.680
for people who wanted more information

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:05.600
from the laboratory about
the viruses they do have,

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:07.750
and what work they've
been doing with them.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:11.000
- [Narrator] Then, there
was another allegation

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:14.760
about the institute that
surfaced more than a year later.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:18.310
- What US intelligence officials learned

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:21.100
is that there were three researchers

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.670
who became ill enough
to seek hospital care.

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:25.640
They had symptoms that were like COVID,

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:29.390
but the symptoms like COVID
are also like flu symptoms.

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:32.840
And flu was definitely
circulating in Wuhan at that time.

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:34.710
- [Narrator] Four days after
the intelligence report

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:37.900
about the sick lab workers, the
Biden administration ordered

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:41.260
a US intelligence inquiry
into the two scenarios.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:43.314
China said the report was untrue.

00:08:43.314 --> 00:08:47.231
(speaking in foreign language)

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:49.830
- Some people don't believe China

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:52.050
and say this has to be a coverup.

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:55.330
Others say we just need to verify,

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:56.750
though that's what we do in science.

00:08:56.750 --> 00:09:00.680
We verify, we look at the data
and reach our own conclusion.

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:04.710
It really does boil down to
a question of transparency.

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:06.250
- [Narrator] So for
any progress to be made

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:07.940
in the origins of the virus,

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:10.140
it'll ultimately require more data,

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:13.492
and fuller access to where it
was first detected in China.

00:09:13.492 --> 00:09:16.075
(bright music)

